### PAYLOAD REGISTRY
{
 "standing": "Normative closed registry for every registered PAYLOAD in this graph. It resolves the question a payload schema digest alone cannot: which document bytes are hashed and which selector validates. It is the single mapping identity-and-evidence section 3 names; nothing in this graph admits a payload under a caller-chosen or guessed schema.",
 "law": {
  "payloadSchemaDigest": "raw SHA-256 of the EXACT FULL schema DOCUMENT file bytes named by the row",
  "validation": "by the row SELECTOR against that document, resolved through the pinned local registry closure with no network retrieval. A payload is NEVER validated against a whole multi-record bundle, and a bundle is never required to carry a root type: the row, not the document shape, decides admission.",
  "codec": "C (section 3 canonical JSON) for every class below",
  "unregistered": "a key with no row refuses; there is no default row and no caller-selected schema",
  "keyedBy": "a key name prefixed `payload.` is read from the parsed payload; every other name is read from the naming record. The key is never caller-selected free text."
 },
 "classes": {
  "relation": {
   "keyedBy": [
    "fact.relation"
   ],
   "document": "foundation/relation-payload-schemas.v2.json",
   "registry": "x-opensip-relation-registry.relations",
   "note": "The 13 rows live in the document itself so relation, selector, universe rule and rung ladder cannot drift from the schemas they describe. fact.resolution must be a rung of the row ladder, the rung required/forbidden field rules apply, and universeRule same-only requires fact.sourceUniverse == fact.targetUniverse."
  },
  "coverage": {
   "keyedBy": [
    "payload.schemaVersion"
   ],
   "rows": {
    "3": {
     "document": "native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json",
     "selector": "#/$defs/CoverageResultV3",
     "owner": "native-evidence section 4"
    }
   }
  },
  "import": {
   "keyedBy": [
    "kind",
    "payload.payloadDomain"
   ],
   "mirrors": "workflows_model.PayloadRegistryV1 / native-evidence section 7.1",
   "rows": {
    "runtime|workflow.import-payload.runtime.v1": {
     "document": "workflows/schemas/imported-evidence.schema.json",
     "selector": "#/$defs/RuntimePayloadV1",
     "owner": "workflow"
    },
    "test|workflow.import-payload.test.v1": {
     "document": "workflows/schemas/test-execution.schema.json",
     "selector": "#/$defs/TestPayloadV1",
     "owner": "workflow"
    },
    "history|workflow.import-payload.history.v1": {
     "document": "workflows/schemas/imported-evidence.schema.json",
     "selector": "#/$defs/HistoryPayloadV1",
     "owner": "workflow"
    },
    "dependency|native.import-payload.dependency-source.v1": {
     "document": "native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json",
     "selector": "#/$defs/DependencySourcePayloadV1",
     "owner": "native"
    },
    "prepared|native.import-payload.prepared-output.v1": {
     "document": "native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json",
     "selector": "#/$defs/PreparedOutputPayloadV1",
     "owner": "native"
    }
   },
   "note": "The Run closure re-derives these rows from the workflow registry and refuses if they differ, so one registry cannot drift from the other."
  },
  "parameter": {
   "keyedBy": [
    "analysis-spec/stage-spec parameter schemaDigest"
   ],
   "rows": {
    "foundation/import-source-context.schema.json": {
     "document": "foundation/import-source-context.schema.json",
     "selector": "#",
     "owner": "foundation"
    },
    "workflows/schemas/policy-document.schema.json#/$defs/ScopeDocumentV1": {
     "document": "workflows/schemas/policy-document.schema.json",
     "selector": "#/$defs/ScopeDocumentV1",
     "owner": "workflows",
     "note": "The workflow glob scope-policy document that comparison-result EvaluationContext.scopeDigest names and that the comparison contract requires to be bound as an analysis-spec parameter. With the class closed to one document that binding refused at Plan admission, so the comparison scope axis could not be populated at all. This row is deliberately a DIFFERENT record from the foundation scope-descriptor that plan.scopeDigest names: scope-descriptor is the repository extent actually walked (workspace roots and path prefixes), ScopeDocumentV1 is the policy's include/exclude glob selection over that extent. Both may appear in one Plan; neither substitutes for the other."
    }
   },
   "note": "An analysis-spec or stage-spec parameter may only cite a document on this closed list. That replaces the withdrawn \"a payload schema document must constrain the payload directly\" rule, which contradicted the native and workflow bundle registries. A cited schema with no row refuses: there is no default row, no caller-selected schema and no permissive fallback for an unregistered parameter.",
   "keyIsTheDocumentNotThePair": "Normative accounting of a bounded limitation, retained deliberately (CB4-ADV-4). The key is the cited schemaDigest, which is the SHA-256 of the whole DOCUMENT's bytes, not a (document, selector) pair. Both rows are distinguishable today - import-source-context.schema.json and policy-document.schema.json are different documents with different digests - so there is no ambiguity in this release. The limitation is structural and is stated rather than left to be rediscovered: a SECOND parameter row selecting another $def out of a document an existing row already cites would be indistinguishable by this key, and the first listed would otherwise silently win. That case is FORBIDDEN and refuses (PAYLOAD_PARAMETER_AMBIGUOUS_ROW), which is the registry law declining to make an arbitrary selection rather than a defect. Note that the same policy-document.schema.json bytes are also named by plan.policyDigest and plan.waiverDigest, but under the `canonical-record` representation and a different payload CLASS, so those are not in this key space and raise no collision.",
   "whyNotRekeyByDocumentAndSelector": "Re-keying the class by (document, selector) would lift the restriction and would change no identity today, and it is deliberately NOT done here. No existing requirement needs a second $def out of one document as a parameter; adding flexibility no requirement asks for would widen the closed key space and the admission surface for a case that does not exist, and would retire an explicit refusal in exchange for a rule that has never been exercised. If such a requirement ever arises it is an explicit versioned design correction with its own controls - not a silent widening, and not something an implementer may assume."
  }
 }
